// The conformance corpus: what tutuca script MEANS, as data.
//
// One AST, three backends — the interpreter that ships today, the MoonBit
// emitter, and wasm-gc when there is an encoder to emit into. A language with
// three implementations and no shared corpus does not have one semantics, it
// has three, and the difference shows up as a component that behaves
// differently depending on how it was built.
//
// So the cases are DATA and nothing here runs them. Each backend writes an
// adapter — the interpreter's is `run_corpus_test.mbt` beside it, the emitter's
// generates a module and drives it through `@harness` — and a case that one
// backend passes and another does not is the thing the corpus exists to catch.
//
// Effects are compared as their PRINTED form rather than as a type, for the
// same reason: `@interp.Effect` is one backend's spelling of them, and a corpus
// that named it could only ever be checked against that backend.
import {
  "marianoguerra/tutuca/core" @tutuca,
}
